llmlang <command>: the compiler's own CLI entry point
	- accepts no arguments; reads argv directly
	- parses argv into a command and validated flags, then dispatches to check, build, or finalize
	- --help or -h anywhere in argv, or no arguments at all, prints usage
	- --version prints the installed package version, falling back to a bundled version string when llmlang isn't actually installed
	- rejects a command outside {check, build, finalize}, and rejects any flag that isn't valid for the given command (--coverage on build, say, or --config on finalize) - nothing is silently ignored
	- --config and --output-format each consume the next argument as their value
	- for check case:
		→ call llmlang.cli.run_check
			- accepts a list of file or directory paths, the parsed flags, an optional config path override, and the output format
			- checks every path given, applying config-resolved coverage and exclusions, and reports every failure together
			→ call llmlang.cli.load_config
				- accepts a starting directory and an optional explicit config path override
				- loads the [tool.llmlang] table from pyproject.toml, degrading gracefully when it can't be read
				- checks whether an override path was given:
					- if yes, uses it directly
					- if no, finds the nearest pyproject.toml from the starting directory
				- checks whether the override path doesn't exist, or this Python has no tomllib (needs 3.11+):
					- if yes, warns to stderr and returns an empty config, rather than crashing
				- checks whether no pyproject.toml was found at all:
					- if yes, falls back to an empty config, with no warning
			←---- returns the [tool.llmlang] table
			→ call llmlang.cli.resolve_coverage
				- accepts the parsed flags and the loaded config
				- decides whether --coverage is active, letting a CLI flag override the config either way
				- --no-coverage always forces it off, then --coverage always forces it on, then the config's own coverage value is the default
			←---- returns whether coverage checking is active
			- accepts any number of file or directory paths, auto-discovering every *.llm file under a directory and checking a .llm file directly
			→ call llmlang.cli.discover_llm_files
				- accepts a root directory and an extra set of directory names to exclude
				- finds every *.llm file under a directory, honoring the built-in and config-configured exclusions
				- excludes .git, __pycache__, node_modules, venv, and .venv by default, plus anything the config's exclude list adds
			←---- returns the sorted list of discovered files
			- warns to stderr and skips a path that doesn't exist, rather than failing the whole run
			- exits with an error if no .llm files are found under any given path
			- repeats checking for each discovered or directly given llmlang file:
				→ call llmlang.cli.check_one
					- accepts an llmlang file path and whether coverage checking is active
					→ call llmlang.lockfile_checker.check
						- accepts an llmlang file path and a lockfile path
						- checks a lockfile by using Parser and Extractor to compare current text and code hashes against Lockfile, reporting each entry as SPEC_DIVERGED, CODE_DIVERGED, or unchanged, alongside its file and line
						- checks whether any test bullet lacks a matching llm-test comment with the same text and canonical node name:
							- if yes, reports TEST_LINK_MISSING
						- checks whether any llm-test comment lacks a matching test bullet with the same text and canonical node name:
							- if yes, reports TEST_TRACE_STALE
						- checks whether a linked test's backing code hash differs from the lockfile:
							- if yes, reports TEST_CODE_DIVERGED
						- checks whether a → call target reachable from an @entry-point entry is unresolved or ambiguous:
							- if yes, reports FLOW_ERROR, naming the entry it came from, without losing any other finding for the same file
						- checks whether the checked-in .llmflow matches what generating one fresh from the current llmlang and code would produce:
							- if no, reports FLOW_FILE_STALE
						- checks whether an entry's own llmlang text changed, it's brand new, or an upstream policy covering it changed:
							- if yes, reports it as SPEC_DIVERGED
						- checks whether an entry's handle is missing from the code or its code changed unexpectedly:
							- if yes, reports it as CODE_DIVERGED
						- reports a codebase with none of these changes as fully clean
						- never prints anything itself; every finding is returned as a Finding record in a list, so a caller can present every failure from a run together instead of relying on interleaved prints, or render them as structured data instead of text
						- each finding names a file and line where known: entry-level findings point at the entry's header line in the llmlang file, code-level findings point at the handle's own comment line in the source file
					←---- returns whether everything checked out clean, the set of named-entry tracking keys currently flagged for any reason, and the list of findings
					- if checking raises an error, reports it as a single ERROR finding instead of letting the exception propagate
					- checks whether coverage checking is active:
						- if yes:
							→ call llmlang.coverage_checker.check_coverage
								- accepts an llmlang file path
								- repeats for each file the llmlang tree knows about:
									- checks whether the file has a Python extension:
										- if no, skips it, not flagged
										- if yes, continues processing
									- only considers functions defined at module or class level; a function nested inside another function is never checked on its own, since it has no stable qualified name to report or exempt, and its enclosing function's own handle (if any) already sweeps its code into that handle's region
									- repeats for each such function:
										- checks whether a comment handle, or a bare "[llm-exempt]" comment, sits on the line directly above it or above its first decorator (a reason may be written before the exempt tag on the same line but isn't required, and there is no built-in exemption for dunder methods, private helpers, or anything else):
											- if yes, continues to the next function
											- if no, reports it as UNMAPPED_CODE
							←---- returns the list of findings, each a Finding record
						- if no, skips coverage checking
				←---- returns whether it checked out clean and the combined list of findings
			- prints "checking X" progress and any warnings to stderr, so stdout stays clean for the report in either format
			- for json case:
				→ call llmlang.cli.print_report_json
					- accepts every checked file's findings, keyed by file
					- prints every finding as JSON, structured identically to what the text report renders from
					- the JSON shape is {"ok": bool, "results": {file: [{category, message, file, line}, ...]}}
			- for text case:
				→ call llmlang.cli.print_report_text
					- accepts every checked file's findings, keyed by file
					- prints every finding across every checked file in one delineated FAILURES section, or a bare OK line when nothing was flagged
					- reports the total failure and file count at the end, correctly pluralized
			- exits 0 if every checked file was clean, 1 otherwise
	- for build case:
		→ call llmlang.cli.run_build
			- accepts a single llmlang file path
			- builds a fresh lockfile for exactly one llmlang file, unguarded
			- refuses more than one file argument, since build is always scoped to one project
			→ call llmlang.lockfile_builder.build
				- accepts an llmlang file path and a lockfile output path
				- builds a lockfile by using Parser to read llmlang, Extractor to locate each source-handled entry's code, and Lockfile to record a text and code hash per entry
				- writes test trace records with the llm-test explanation text, relative path, and backing code hash
				- generates and overwrites the sibling .llmflow file from the current @entry-point entries and bullets
				- checks whether any test bullet lacks a matching llm-test comment or any llm-test comment lacks a matching test bullet:
					- if yes, refuses to write anything
				- checks whether any → call target in an @entry-point entry's reachable bullets is unresolved or ambiguous:
					- if yes, refuses to write anything
				- has no notion of dispositions or a change manifest at all; never reads or writes anything but the lockfile and the generated flow file
				- reproduces the same hashes when run again against an unchanged codebase
			←---- returns the built lock data
			- exits 1 and prints the error on failure
	- for finalize case:
		→ call llmlang.cli.run_finalize
			- accepts a single llmlang file path
			- finalizes exactly one llmlang file's lockfile and change manifest, guarded by Checker
			- refuses more than one file argument, since finalize is always scoped to one project
			→ call llmlang.lockfile_builder.finalize
				- accepts an llmlang file path, a lockfile output path, and a change manifest file path
				- uses Checker to find every named entry currently flagged against the existing lockfile, and refuses to write anything unless the change manifest covers every one of them with a non-empty disposition
				- checks whether any → call target in an @entry-point entry's reachable bullets is unresolved or ambiguous, same as build:
					- if yes, refuses to write anything
				- generates and overwrites the sibling .llmflow file from the current @entry-point entries and bullets, same as build; a flow-only edit needs no disposition of its own, since it can only exist as part of an entry's own bullets, already gated by that entry's own SPEC_DIVERGED
				- accepts a fresh disposition in the manifest as a plain string, and stamps it with the entry's current spec hash and code hash
				- a spec hash combines the entry's own text with every policy currently covering it, so a disposition goes stale whether the entry's own text changed or an applicable policy did
				- for a manifest entry that's already stamped, keeps it only if both hashes still match the entry's current state; otherwise drops it as stale, along with any entry whose key no longer exists
				- builds every hash and disposition fresh from the current llmlang text, current code, current policies, and current manifest only; never carries anything forward from the lockfile's own prior content
				- writes both the new lockfile and the updated (stamped and pruned) manifest file, but never alters a disposition's own text
			←---- returns the built lock data
			- exits 1 and prints the error on failure
